## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a small, secure, observable event-driven pipeline using core GCP primitives: Audit Logs, Pub/Sub, Cloud Functions, and BigQuery partitioning, with idempotent writes and minimal IAM.

## Key Concepts

- Cloud Audit Logs for IAM policy changes
- Pub/Sub as ingestion layer
- Cloud Functions for lightweight processing
- BigQuery partitioning by date and insertId for idempotency
- Minimal IAM bindings and test strategy

## Code Example

```javascript
// Cloud Function skeleton: Pub/Sub -> BigQuery
const {BigQuery} = require('@google-cloud/bigquery');
const bigquery = new BigQuery();
exports.processIamPolicyChange = async (event, context) => {
  const log = JSON.parse(Buffer.from(event.data, 'base64').toString());
  const insertId = log.log_id || log.id;
  await bigquery
    .dataset('audit')
    .table('iam_changes')
    .insert([{ insertId, event_type: log.policy_change?.type, resource: log.resource, timestamp: log.timestamp }]);
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test end-to-end with simulated logs and verify idempotency?
- How would you handle late-arriving logs or schema drift?

